    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_75e30a276d03ee7136ad771dc2c616bc5f8c8f221632395043e44c0f15441a23",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The global theorem and the finite computations answer different questions. Theorem 2 states pointwise convergence zeta_q(s) to zeta(s) as q approaches one for every s except the pole at one. The reported truncations are moderately accurate at s=1/2 but, near the illustrated nontrivial zero, pass through extremely large intermediate values and change sharply with truncation length before becoming small. These examples demonstrate severe truncation sensitivity and a long pre-asymptotic regime in the cases shown, without identifying the numerical mechanism or establishing a general conditioning theorem.",
