    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8c3888af510792ce88b3b745feb282f467920f50b8bd2577163a966764b2a140",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The classical Euler-Maclaurin continuation works because increasing the remainder order shifts its integrability boundary, whereas direct differentiation of the q-kernel retains q^{x(s-1)} and remains confined to Re(s)>1. Expanding the periodic Bernoulli factor in Fourier modes and substituting q^x=u produces incomplete beta integrals; repeated integration by parts now shifts beta parameters and extends the half-plane, and the q-to-1 limit reconstructs the classical Euler-Maclaurin remainder term by term.",
